1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,282
Black holes: places in the universe where not even light can escape

2
00:00:06,282 --> 00:00:09,962
At the heart of the Milky Way sits a black hole...

3
00:00:09,962 --> 00:00:13,898
...as heavy as about 4 million Suns

4
00:00:13,898 --> 00:00:18,752
And when two black holes collided...

5
00:00:18,752 --> 00:00:23,490
...for an instant they released more power than all the stars in the universe

6
00:00:23,490 --> 00:00:29,478
But their story begins... on a battlefield

7
00:00:29,495 --> 00:00:33,222
DECEMBER 1915

8
00:00:33,222 --> 00:00:36,624
On the Russian front, a German army lieutenant...

9
00:00:36,624 --> 00:00:40,107
...worked out the paths of artillery shells

10
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:43,068
His name was Karl Schwarzschild...

11
00:00:43,068 --> 00:00:46,980
One of Germany's most famous astronomers

12
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:52,332
Amid the noise of war...

13
00:00:52,332 --> 00:00:53,667
EINSTEIN'S GENERAL RELATIVITY

14
00:00:53,667 --> 00:00:56,755
...he found the first exact solution of its equations

15
00:00:56,755 --> 00:00:58,601
Einstein himself was amazed

16
00:00:58,601 --> 00:01:01,597
But the solution hid a strange boundary

17
00:01:01,597 --> 00:01:06,600
If all of an object's mass is squeezed into a small ball...

18
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,607
...nothing can ever come back out of it

19
00:01:09,607 --> 00:01:10,838
Not even light

20
00:01:10,838 --> 00:01:14,541
For the Sun, that ball would be just 6 km wide

21
00:01:14,541 --> 00:01:17,165
For Earth, the size of a marble

22
00:01:17,165 --> 00:01:20,811
Five months later, Schwarzschild was dead

23
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:27,811
He never knew what his calculation was hiding...

24
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:29,943
BLACK HOLE

25
00:01:29,943 --> 00:01:32,614
More than 100 years later...

26
00:01:32,614 --> 00:01:34,761
2026

27
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:39,080
The James Webb Space Telescope found a black hole...

28
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,713
...that may be older than its own galaxy

29
00:01:47,113 --> 00:01:49,675
But the idea was even older than Schwarzschild

30
00:01:49,675 --> 00:01:53,668
1783

31
00:01:53,668 --> 00:01:55,247
The village priest of Thornhill, England...

32
00:01:55,247 --> 00:01:57,035
...John Michell

33
00:01:57,035 --> 00:02:00,496
...wrote a long letter to his friend Henry Cavendish

34
00:02:00,496 --> 00:02:03,131
Michell also studied earthquakes...

35
00:02:03,131 --> 00:02:08,181
...and his balance was later used to weigh the Earth

36
00:02:08,181 --> 00:02:12,245
Using Newton's gravity, he asked a simple question

37
00:02:12,245 --> 00:02:14,996
What if a star were as dense as the Sun...

38
00:02:14,996 --> 00:02:18,329
...but 500 times wider?

39
00:02:18,329 --> 00:02:20,117
The answer was shocking

40
00:02:20,117 --> 00:02:25,717
Even light would be too slow to escape its gravity

41
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:28,411
Such a star would be completely invisible

42
00:02:28,411 --> 00:02:29,699
Michell called them...

43
00:02:29,699 --> 00:02:30,976
DARK STARS

44
00:02:30,976 --> 00:02:37,954
They could be found only by the stars orbiting them

45
00:02:37,954 --> 00:02:43,884
About 200 years later, astronomers did exactly that

